<onlyinclude>'''Electronic design automation''' (EDA) means using software to help engineers design and check electronic circuits before they are built.</onlyinclude>

== What does it do? ==
EDA also helps engineers design programmable hardware, such as FPGAs. It does not manufacture the chips itself.

Imagine designing a chip with millions of tiny switches. EDA software helps translate a description of what the circuit should do into a connected layout of logic elements and wires. Other tools simulate the design, check whether signals arrive on time, and detect layout problems.

== A typical chip-design journey ==
# Describe the intended circuit and its behavior.
# Test the description using simulation and other verification methods.
# Convert it into logic gates and registers (synthesis).
# Arrange the elements and connect their wires (placement and routing).
# Check timing and physical design rules, revise, and repeat.

The steps overlap: engineers repeatedly return to earlier stages when a check fails. FPGA designs follow a related but different path because the device's circuitry is configurable.

== What it cannot promise ==
A completed software flow is not proof that a chip will work under every condition. Physical manufacturing, packaging, electrical testing, and production yield are separate concerns.

